"==English==\n{{was wotd|2007|October|12}}\n{{wp}}\n\n[[Image:Kantoutanpopo.jpg|thumb|A dandelion in flower]]\n[[Image:A dandelion.jpg|thumb|A seeding dandelion]]\n\n===Etymology===\n{{root|en|ine-pro|*h\u2083ed- (bite)}}\nFrom {{inh|en|enm|dentdelyon}}, from {{der|en|fro|dent de lion||lion's tooth}}, also in {{der|en|la|d\u0113ns le\u014dnis}}, referring to the jagged shape of the plant's leaves. The term is now rare in French (together with ''liondent'', calque from Germanic), but compare {{cog|es|diente de le\u00f3n}}, {{cog|pt|dente-de-le\u00e3o}}, {{cog|it|dente di leone}}, {{cog|de|L\u00f6wenzahn}}, {{cog|nb|l\u00f8vetann}}, {{cog|cy|dant y llew}}, all descendants, calques, or loan translations of the Latin term. {{etydate|late 14th c.}}\n\nDisplaced various words, including native {{ncog|ang|\u01e3\u0121wyrt}}, native {{ncog|enm|houndes-tooth}}, and {{ncog|en|tell-time}} and {{m|en|swine's snout}}; and mostly displaced {{ncog|en|cankerwort}}, {{m|en|monk's head}}, and {{m|en|pissabed}}.\n\n===Pronunciation===\n* {{IPA|en|/\u02c8d\u00e6n.d\u0259\u02ccla\u026a.\u0259n/|/\u02c8d\u00e6n.di\u02ccla\u026a.\u0259n/|a=GA}}\n* {{IPA|en|/\u02c8d\u00e6n.d\u026a\u02ccla\u026a.\u0259n/|/\u02c8d\u00e6n.d\u0259\u02ccla\u026a.\u0259n/|a=RP}}\n* {{audio|en|en-uk-dandelion.ogg|a=UK}}\n* {{audio|en|En-us-dandelion.ogg|a=US}}\n* {{audio|en|en-au-dandelion.ogg|a=AU}}\n\n===Noun===\n{{en-noun|~}}\n\n# {{lb|en|countable}} Any of the several [[species]] of [[plant]] in the genus {{taxfmt|Taraxacum|genus}}, characterised by [[yellow]] [[flower heads]] and notched, broad-ended [[leaf|leaves]], especially the [[common dandelion]] ({{taxfmt|Taraxacum officinale|species}}).\n#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|author=Joseph Richardson Parke|title=The Wizard of the Damavant: A Tale of the Crusades|page=151|text=the sanguine hue of the poppy and the hibiscus, the gold of the daisy and '''dandelion''', the dark green of the sorrage on either side, and the blue and purple of the blossoming mulberry and sycamore}}\n#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Launer|title=Do We Even Need Men?|magazine=w:Literary Hub|url=https://lithub.com/do-we-even-need-men/|date=2018-04-24|passage=There are asexual variants among all sorts of creatures, including jellyfish, '''dandelions''', lichens and lizards.}}\n# {{lb|en|countable}} The [[flower]] [[head]] or fruiting head of the dandelion plant.\n# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[yellow]] [[colour]], like that of the flower.\n#: {{color panel|EFD600}}\n\n====Hyponyms====\n* {{taxlink|Taraxacum albidum|species}}, a white-flowering [[Japanese]] dandelion.\n* {{taxlink|Taraxacum californicum|species}}, the endangered [[California]] dandelion.\n* {{taxlink|Taraxacum japonicum|species}}, Japanese dandelion. No ring of smallish, downward-turned leaves under the flowerhead.\n* {{taxlink|Taraxacum kok-saghyz|species}}, [[Russian]] dandelion, which produces rubber.\n* {{taxlink|Taraxacum laevigatum|species}}, red-seeded dandelion; achenes reddish brown and leaves deeply cut throughout length. Inner bracts' tips are hooded.\n** {{taxlink|Taraxacum erythrospermum|species}}, often considered a variety of {{taxlink|Taraxacum laevigatum|species}}.\n* {{taxfmt|Taraxacum officinale|species}} (syn. {{taxlink|Taraxacum officinale subsp. vulgare|subspecies}}), [[common dandelion]]. Found in many forms.\n\n====Derived terms====\n{{col|en|dandelionlike|dwarf dandelion|dandelion clock|dandelion greens|dandelion salad|dandelion seed|dandelion wine|dandelion yellow|dandelion and burdock|dandelion coffee|dandelion tea|desert dandelion| blue dandelion|fall dandelion|Kazakh dandelion|Russian dandelion|Italian dandelion|Bolander's mock dandelion|false dandelion}}\n\n====Translations====\n{{trans-top|plant, wild flower of the genus ''Taraxacum''}}\n* Albanian: {{t+|sq|luleradhiqe}}\n* Amharic: {{t|am|\u12f3\u1295\u12f4\u120a\u12ee\u1295}}\n* Arabic: {{t|ar|\u0647\u0650\u0646\u0652\u062f\u0650\u0628\u064e\u0627\u0621|f}}, {{t|ar|\u0637\u064e\u0631\u0652\u062e\u064e\u0634\u0652\u0642\u064f\u0648\u0646|m}}, {{t+|ar|\u0645\u064f\u0631\u0651|m}} {{q|obsolete}}\n* Armenian: {{t+|hy|\u056d\u0561\u057f\u0578\u0582\u057f\u056b\u056f}}\n* Asturian: {{t|ast|carde\u00f1a|f}}, {{t|ast|cardencha|f}}, {{t|ast|llech\u00f3n|m}}, {{t+|ast|xiblata|f}}, {{t|ast|tarriellu|m}} {{q|Western}}\n* Avar: {{t|av|\u0445\u04cf\u0430\u043c\u0430\u0433\u0430\u0433\u0430}}\n* Azerbaijani: {{t|az|z\u0259ncirotu}}\n* Bashkir: {{t|ba|\u0431\u04d9\u043f\u0435\u043c\u0431\u04d9}}\n* Basque: {{t|eu|txikoria-belar}}, {{t|eu|sorgin-belar}}, {{t|eu|mando-belar}}\n* Belarusian: {{t|be|\u0430\u0434\u0443\u0432\u0430\u0301\u043d\u0447\u044b\u043a|m}}, {{t|be|\u0434\u0437\u044c\u043c\u0443\u0445\u0430\u0301\u0432\u0435\u0446|m}}\n* Bulgarian: {{t|bg|\u0433\u043b\u0443\u0445\u0430\u0440\u0447\u0435|n}}\n* Buryat: {{t|bua|\u043d\u044f\u043c\u043d\u044f\u0430 \u0441\u044d\u0441\u044d\u0433}}, {{t|bua|\u043d\u044f\u043c\u043d\u044f\u0430\u0445\u0430\u0439}}\n* Catalan: {{t+|ca|dent de lle\u00f3|m}}\n* Chechen: {{t|ce|\u0431\u0430\u043f\u043f\u0430}}\n* Chinese:\n*: Cantonese: {{t|yue|\u84b2\u516c\u82f1|tr=pou4 gung1 jing1}}\n*: Mandarin: {{t+|cmn|\u84b2\u516c\u82f1|tr=p\u00fag\u014dngy\u012bng}}\n* Cornish: {{t|kw|dans lew|m}}\n* Cree:\n*: Plains Cree: {{t|crk|\u1405\u14f5\u140b\u1427\u1431\u146f\u14c0\u1422}}, {{t|crk|os\u00e2w\u00e2pikones}}\n* Czech: {{t+|cs|pampeli\u0161ka|f}}\n* Danish: {{t+|da|m\u00e6lkeb\u00f8tte|c}}, {{t|da|l\u00f8vetand|c}}\n* Dargwa: {{t|dar|\u043a\u044a\u0430\u043a\u044a\u0430\u0436\u0443\u0436\u0443}}\n* Dutch: {{t+|nl|paardenbloem}}\n* Esperanto: {{t+|eo|leondento}}, {{t|eo|leontodo}}\n* Estonian: {{t+|et|v\u00f5ilill}}\n* Faroese: {{t|fo|hagas\u00f3lja|f}}, {{t|fo|v\u00e1rhagas\u00f3lja|f}}, {{t|fo|tyrilshattur|m}}\n* Finnish: {{t+|fi|voikukka}}\n* French: {{t+|fr|pissenlit|m}}, {{t+|fr|dent-de-lion|m}} {{qualifier|Switzerland}}, {{t+|fr|baraban|m}}\n* Galician: {{t|gl|dente-de-le\u00f3n|m}}, {{t+|gl|mexac\u00e1n|m}}\n* Georgian: {{t|ka|\u10d1\